ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: green
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS: ["Greedy-cache-aware is by construction the recomputed-prefill argmin under a shared LRU+pinned-while-inflight cache: it always picks the most-resident family, so any policy that ever deviates (bounded-W forcing admission of a starved, cold-family request after W skips) MUST strictly increase recomputed prefill in expectation. The 0/36 joint-dominance result is therefore predicted by basic queueing/cache theory, not an empirical surprise — it is the expected exponent of a constrained optimum vs its unconstrained relaxation (Lagrangian duality: adding a fairness constraint W<inf to a reuse-maximization problem cannot improve the reuse objective).", "Greedy mean-dominating VTC in 31/36 cells is mechanically explained by Zipf skew s>=1.5: hot families both (i) carry most of the arrival mass and (ii) have the most resident blocks, so 'serve hottest first' is approximately 'serve most-arriving first', which approximates fair-by-arrival-rate. This is a known property of skewed workloads, not evidence of a scheduling insight.", "bounded-W beating VTC in 36/36 is largely because VTC's virtual-counter ignores reuse: under service-time feedback with PREFILL_COEF>0, VTC pays *both* fairness *and* prefill (longer service from cache misses lengthens queues), so any cache-aware policy with a non-trivial fairness cap will dominate it. This is structural, not a tuning win."]
CONFOUNDS: ["Service-time feedback coefficients (BASE_SERVICE=0.5, PREFILL_COEF=0.0015, DECODE=0.0008) are fixed point estimates — the coupling strength between prefill and p99 is set by their ratio; the 0/36 result may be specific to this regime. A sensitivity sweep over PREFILL_COEF (e.g., 0.5x, 2x, 4x) would test whether the negative is structural or coefficient-dependent.", "M=400 requests per run is short relative to LRU warmup + Zipf tail coverage at K=16; transient regime may dominate steady-state for cold families, inflating recomputed-prefill variance for non-greedy policies (which admit colder families more often). Run-length sensitivity not reported.", "Cache cap sweep {B+1, B+2, 2B} families is narrow; at 2B the cache is nearly unconstrained (greedy's reuse advantage shrinks) and at B+1 it is brutally tight (greedy's advantage maximizes). The 0/36 may collapse to a non-zero count under a regime not sampled (e.g., cap=4B with tight B).", "Bonferroni m=864 across 36 cells x 2 baselines x 2 axes x 6 W-values is the most conservative correction; BH was promised as secondary but the headline only cites Bonferroni. BH count could change the 'PARTIAL' framing materially."]
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED: ["The simulator assumes per-request service is linear in recomputed_prefill_tokens — real prefill on GPUs is sublinear via chunked prefill and paged KV (acknowledged in RESULTS but not modeled). The negative result's robustness to non-linear prefill cost is asserted, not tested.", "No SLO/priority class structure tested in L0 even though the researcher's own pivot identifies this as the regime where bounded-W could earn its keep. The L0 negative therefore cannot speak to the (likely) production-relevant claim.", "W=0 collapse to FCFS-order and W=inf collapse to greedy are stated but the boundary behavior (W=1) is not separately analyzed — useful as a sanity check that the policy is correctly implemented across the budget spectrum."]
STATISTICAL_GAPS: ["24 seeds with pstdev reported but no per-cell power analysis: at p99=12.75 ± 9.12 (greedy) the CV ~0.71, n=24 gives MDE on delta-p99 of roughly 0.6*sigma ~5.5 time-units at alpha=5.79e-5 — adequate to detect the large effects but borderline for the 13/36 marginal p99 wins. A power table per axis would close this.", "Bootstrap is paired across policies (correct) but resample count varies '4000-5000' — should be fixed and reported. Bonferroni m=864 may swallow real but small joint-dominance effects; an Adaptive BH or hierarchical procedure (test joint-dom only in cells where marginal CIs already exclude 0) would be more powerful without inflating FWER."]
RATIONALE: The headline negative is well-supported and, more importantly, *predicted by elementary theory*: greedy is the argmin of a reuse-maximization program; adding a fairness constraint cannot improve the reuse objective (Lagrangian duality). The committee correctly identified this as a structural, not empirical, finding ("Greedy is the prefill FLOOR in 32/36 cells... You cannot beat the reuse-maximizer on reuse with a fairness cap that *forces* cache-suboptimal admissions"). Pre-registration is genuine (committed 6d100f0 pre-run), seeds=24 exceeds the committee's n=20 floor, service-time feedback is correctly implemented (coupling p99 and prefill via PREFILL_COEF=0.0015), and Bonferroni FWER across m=864 is conservative-correct. The PARTIAL (bounded-W dominates VTC 36/36) is honestly framed as "dominance over the weaker baseline only" — exactly the anti-coping the rules require. A clean negative on the literal claim, with the researcher's own multi-SLO pivot named as the next required_evidence, is a textbook valid outcome — NOT inflate-worthy and NOT GPU-pass-worthy until the metric is reframed to SLO-attainment-at-fixed-prefill on a real scheduler (SGLang RadixAttention + real VTC on vLLM/APC, per RESULTS §"What a GPU pass should measure"). Vote GREEN on the *negative verdict* — the reasoning is sound, the mundane explanation (constrained-optimum can't beat unconstrained-optimum on the unconstrained objective) is correctly identified by the authors themselves, and there is no theory-side reason to suspect a confound is masking a real positive.
